From: Hamad Mota Kalaf <hamad.kalaf@itamaraty.gov.br>
Sent: Thursday 1 October 2026 2:40 pm
To: Events <events@dublincity.ie>
Cc: Marcelo Santa Cruz de Freitas Ferraz <marcelo.ferraz@itamaraty.gov.br>;=
 Jo=E3o Marcelo Costa Melo <joao.c.melo@itamaraty.gov.br>
Subject: Re: Embassy of Brazil. Brazilian Elections. Possible Second Round =
(25/10). Conflict with Marathon.

Dear Siobh=E1n,

Thank you for thoughtful reply. We really appreciate it. With few exception=
s that has been my experience in trying to solve that question.

You can forward my email to the Dublin Marathon group and put them in touch=
 with us, but I fear there will be no need for that.

We have already thoroughly engaged with the Garda and identified possibilit=
ies for Road Access. Inspector Wogan at Donnybrook Garda Station informed m=
e that Donnybrook Road and Anglesea Road will be open throughout the day. T=
herefore, we will divert the entrance gate to Anglesea Road. Electors will,=
 therefore, be able to access by car.

Our main concern at this stage is the provision of adequate public transpor=
tation and Dublin Bus was somewhat dismissful of our concerns and sometimes=
 misconstrued them.

That is the reason I am trying to engage with you - as instructed, as well,=
 by Inspector Wogan.

We would like to enquire about two possibilities:


  1.
Will the bus routes that normally go through Donnybrook Road work normally =
on 25 October? Will they stop at stations Donnybrook Garda Station (774), V=
ictoria Avenue (758) and Donnybrook Stadium (759)?
  2.
What would be the recommended passageway for electors arriving at Sandymoun=
t Train Station?


I know you are not the ones responsible for providing these replies, but we=
 would very thankful if you could forward that concern for Dublin Bus and p=
rovide us with a contact that will engage with us.

From our standpoint, the problem is almost solved.

Again I thank you for your collaboration.

Kindest regards,

Hamad


